1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
 - So welcome to our lecture on public key encryption,

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,640
 where we're going to go back to confidentiality.

3
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:09,080
 And so here we go.

4
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:12,000
 If you recall, we've been having these two topics

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,640
 that have been our theme throughout.

6
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:19,640
 Just some grizzly, oh sorry, I'm starting to talk in ROT13.

7
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:24,040
 Let's translate this back to non-ROT13.

8
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:26,640
 The terminology, the two kind of themes we've been following

9
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:29,440
 over the last couple of lectures in this lecture

10
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:31,320
 are confidentiality and integrity.

11
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:34,800
 And confidentiality is hiding, right?

12
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:37,560
 Shielding information, not leaking information to people

13
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:39,280
 that you don't want to show it to.

14
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:41,080
 And integrity is making sure

15
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:42,240
 that you know who you're dealing with.

16
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:45,200
 And then the previous lecture, we really talked about

17
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:47,760
 kind of real light, approachable ways

18
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:51,160
 of ensuring confidentiality with things like Caesar Cipher,

19
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:54,200
 and then integrity using a simple message digest

20
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:56,040
 that based on a shared secret.

21
00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:58,680
 So the problem with all of those things

22
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:02,720
 that we just saw is that they require a shared secret.

23
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:04,560
 And the problem in the world of the internet

24
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:07,400
 is it's just really difficult for every one of us

25
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:09,520
 before we establish,

26
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:16,360
 before we can make any purchases or whatever at Amazon

27
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:19,680
 that we somehow have to drive to Amazon headquarters

28
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:22,480
 and get a shared secret from Amazon.

29
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:26,040
 They'll open up a book and say, okay, hi Chuck,

30
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:27,960
 I see who you are and here's our shared secret.

31
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:29,760
 And you walk away and as long as you carry

32
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:31,960
 that shared secret, away you go.

33
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:33,800
 And if the shared secret is lost,

34
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:36,040
 it's difficult to review both.

35
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:38,840
 So as the internet and frankly, in general,

36
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:43,800
 as security needed to be able to work at arm's length,

37
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:46,480
 meaning that you couldn't always bring everybody together

38
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:48,000
 and hand out shared secrets

39
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:49,960
 and then have them go to the far reaches of the world

40
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:54,840
 and communicate, public key encryption was identified

41
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:58,480
 as a extremely elegant solution to this problem.

42
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:02,880
 And so it was proposed by Diffie and Hellman in 1976.

43
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:06,480
 And it relies on two keys, it's asymmetric,

44
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:09,440
 meaning we're not using the same key to encrypt as decrypt

45
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:11,280
 the way we were in the previous lectures,

46
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:12,320
 these are asymmetric.

47
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:14,000
 There is a public key,

48
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,160
 which is actually does not need any protection whatsoever

49
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:18,360
 and a private key.

50
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:20,960
 And the idea is they're generated inside of a computer,

51
00:02:20,960 --> 00:02:23,160
 you generate the public key and the private key,

52
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:24,520
 you send out the public key,

53
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:26,960
 the public key is used to do the encryption

54
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:29,240
 and then private key is used to do the decryption.

55
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:31,160
 And they're related mathematically

56
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:33,400
 in a way that's well understood,

57
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:36,840
 but difficult to compute for a key length that's large enough.

58
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:39,640
 So there's a public key and a private key.

59
00:02:39,640 --> 00:02:42,320
 So I'd like you to take a look at this little video

60
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:45,840
 up on YouTube of Diffie, Hellman and Merkel,

61
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:47,180
 the inventors of this.

62
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:50,880
 And I think it's a great video.

63
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:53,000
 I would love it if this were my video,

64
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:54,360
 but I didn't produce this video.

65
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:56,740
 So take a quick look.

66
00:02:56,740 --> 00:02:59,320
 (gentle music)

67
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:04,960
 So one of the things about this

68
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:07,600
 public private key encryption is now that we know about it,

69
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:09,200
 it's like, whoa, it's pretty obvious.

70
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:11,880
 And frankly, Caesar and the Germans and everybody

71
00:03:11,880 --> 00:03:12,960
 could have used this idea.

72
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:14,740
 They just hadn't thought of it yet.

73
00:03:14,740 --> 00:03:16,360
 And the other thing that's kind of interesting,

74
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:19,200
 if you look into the story of this is that

75
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:21,480
 the first reaction people got

76
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:22,680
 when they start thinking about this

77
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:24,720
 is like, it can't be this easy.

78
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:26,600
 Now it's sort of both easy and hard,

79
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:30,120
 but the concept is real elegant and really beautiful.

80
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:32,320
 And that is that we have this public key.

81
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:34,840
 So the public key is part of a public private pair

82
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:37,200
 and it's used to do the encryption.

83
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:40,200
 The beauty is it's computationally difficult

84
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:41,920
 to recover that private key

85
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:44,980
 from the public key and the encrypted text.

86
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:47,920
 The key thing is it's not impossible.

87
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:51,240
 And that's kind of one of the interesting philosophies

88
00:03:51,240 --> 00:03:53,840
 of security that we started at the very beginning

89
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:54,680
 in talking about security.

90
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:57,760
 The perfect security is kind of impossible to achieve

91
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:01,480
 unless you simply don't send anything.

92
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:05,240
 And so public private key, asymmetric keys

93
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:07,860
 is well understood as to how you would break it.

94
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:09,720
 Everyone knows how to break it.

95
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:11,520
 The problem is, is that computers

96
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:13,400
 aren't fast enough to break it.

97
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:14,760
 And when computers get faster,

98
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:16,300
 it will just make the keys bigger.

99
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:19,880
 So the mathematics of this makes it impractical to break.

100
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:22,640
 I mean, literally impractical to break.

101
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:24,280
 Now I think we can safely assume

102
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:27,680
 that governments probably have enough computation

103
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:29,920
 to crack these once in a great while.

104
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:32,440
 I mean, if they're not cracking every transaction

105
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:35,000
 between you and target when you wanna buy something,

106
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:36,920
 but if they really have to,

107
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:39,480
 they can record the encrypted transmissions.

108
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:41,940
 And if they really had to and took a long time,

109
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:45,000
 I have no idea how long it would be, they can break it.

110
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,280
 So that's actually kind of a neat way to think about this

111
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:50,420
 by revealing it all.

112
00:04:50,420 --> 00:04:52,340
 Frankly, any computer scientist

113
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:55,260
 could make a name for them their whole life

114
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:57,720
 if they proved that there was something wrong with this.

115
00:04:57,720 --> 00:05:01,360
 By revealing the algorithm, revealing the cracking technique,

116
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:03,480
 if someone can come up with a better cracking technique,

117
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:06,240
 it is like fame and glory forever.

118
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:08,740
 Which means that we're pretty sure

119
00:05:08,740 --> 00:05:10,400
 that there's no good way to crack this

120
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,240
 other than the brute force mechanism

121
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:15,400
 that requires a large amount of computation.

122
00:05:15,400 --> 00:05:20,400
 So if you're going to use a public-private key encryption,

123
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:23,280
 you have to generate a pair.

124
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:28,280
 And it starts by choosing two really large random numbers

125
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:34,040
 with hundreds, if not thousands of digits that are prime.

126
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:37,200
 So you kind of choose a random number, really big,

127
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:39,800
 and then you kind of look around for a nearby prime number

128
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:43,160
 and you choose two of those, and then you multiply them.

129
00:05:43,160 --> 00:05:46,280
 Okay, getting an even larger number.

130
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:49,080
 And then through some steps, through some calculations,

131
00:05:49,080 --> 00:05:51,440
 you compute the public and the private keys

132
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:53,120
 from that large number.

133
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:55,440
 The essence of this are those two prime numbers.

134
00:05:55,440 --> 00:05:56,840
 Prime numbers, of course, are numbers

135
00:05:56,840 --> 00:05:59,000
 that you only divide by themselves in one,

136
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:00,800
 which means they have no factors,

137
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:01,960
 which means they're kind of like looking

138
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:03,920
 for a needle in a haystack.

139
00:06:03,920 --> 00:06:05,760
 And so the public and private key

140
00:06:05,760 --> 00:06:07,880
 is really based on these two prime numbers.

141
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:11,480
 If you could figure out what the prime numbers were,

142
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:13,920
 you'd be okay, but the computational difficulty

143
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:16,520
 is finding the prime numbers that are extremely large

144
00:06:16,520 --> 00:06:17,920
 and finding the right prime numbers

145
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:19,600
 that are extremely large.

146
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:22,560
 So it's easy to do some calculations in one direction,

147
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:23,840
 but not in other.

148
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:26,840
 So for example, what are the factors of 55,124,159?

149
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:33,120
 Quick, but if I simply ask you,

150
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:38,120
 what do you multiply 70,919 to get that 55 million number,

151
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:41,800
 that's easy, you do a division.

152
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:46,800
 And it turns out that you can find out 69,61 really easy.

153
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:52,520
 So if I just say, what are these two numbers, that's hard.

154
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:55,760
 If I say, given this number,

155
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:58,160
 what's the other number, that's trivial.

156
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:01,160
 So you can think of this as the decryption

157
00:07:01,160 --> 00:07:03,600
 is where the receiver of the message

158
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:06,160
 knows kind of half of the calculation,

159
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:08,040
 whereas the world doesn't know either half of it,

160
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:10,080
 doesn't know the calculation.

161
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:11,480
 So it has to figure out both halves,

162
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:14,240
 whereas the receiver only has to figure out one half.

163
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:16,440
 And so that's how asking the question

164
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:21,200
 of what are the factors versus given one, what's the other?

165
00:07:21,200 --> 00:07:23,000
 So it takes a problem that's easy,

166
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,600
 makes it computationally nearly impossible.

167
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:28,240
 But again, not impossible, just nearly impossible.

168
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:32,040
 Okay, so here's the notion.

169
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:33,880
 So you're about to type your Visa card

170
00:07:33,880 --> 00:07:38,880
 into a credit card into like Amazon's webpage.

171
00:07:38,880 --> 00:07:42,880
 And so what happens is, is that Amazon has a public key

172
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:45,480
 and a private key that they retain.

173
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:48,280
 And they will send you the public key across a medium,

174
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:52,200
 the internet, they're gonna send this to you somehow.

175
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:57,200
 But the bad guys, Eve or Charlie or whoever they are,

176
00:07:57,200 --> 00:08:03,320
 the bad guys, this is Alice and Bob.

177
00:08:03,320 --> 00:08:05,200
 Eve and Charlie are always looking.

178
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:07,000
 So Eve and Charlie could have intercepted

179
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:08,200
 and you assume that they can.

180
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:09,320
 This is the key.

181
00:08:09,320 --> 00:08:11,160
 Don't try to pretend they can't,

182
00:08:11,160 --> 00:08:13,000
 even though it's very difficult for them to do it,

183
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:14,520
 but you assume they can.

184
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:16,560
 So the public key comes across.

185
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:19,880
 It is simply sent to you as part of the beginning

186
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:22,640
 of establishing a secure connection.

187
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:27,240
 And the bad guys see it too, or gals, they see it too.

188
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:29,320
 So the public key comes to you.

189
00:08:29,320 --> 00:08:34,320
 And then what you do is you encrypt using that public key

190
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:38,040
 and create some encrypted text, ciphertext,

191
00:08:38,040 --> 00:08:40,880
 which you then send back across the danger

192
00:08:40,880 --> 00:08:43,800
 where Eve and Charlie are watching.

193
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:46,600
 And it comes across, they intercept the encrypted text.

194
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:48,560
 They've intercepted the public key

195
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:53,560
 and they can try as hard as they like with supercomputers

196
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:55,080
 to derive this.

197
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:56,400
 And frankly, like I said,

198
00:08:56,400 --> 00:08:57,800
 if they had months and months and months

199
00:08:57,800 --> 00:09:01,240
 and really fast computers, they could, okay?

200
00:09:01,240 --> 00:09:04,800
 But because Amazon is in sole possession of the private key

201
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:07,280
 and it never left Amazon's servers,

202
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:10,320
 it is a very simple matter for Amazon to decrypt

203
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:11,240
 and get your plain text.

204
00:09:11,240 --> 00:09:13,200
 It happens very quickly.

205
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:15,120
 Just like if you kind of know half

206
00:09:15,120 --> 00:09:16,920
 of the prime number calculation,

207
00:09:16,920 --> 00:09:19,800
 figuring out the other prime number is really, really easy.

208
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:24,560
 Okay, so again, these people see all this information

209
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:27,680
 and yet it is computationally virtually impossible

210
00:09:27,680 --> 00:09:30,200
 for all practical purposes to do it.

211
00:09:30,200 --> 00:09:32,800
 And so it's beautiful because there was no need

212
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:33,960
 to protect the public key.

213
00:09:33,960 --> 00:09:37,320
 We never had to get in the same room and away it goes.

214
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:40,360
 So you just, Amazon just blasts out its public key

215
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:42,600
 and we encrypt using Amazon's public key.

216
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:46,100
 We can't decrypt it, but we don't need to decrypt it.

217
00:09:46,100 --> 00:09:50,440
 All we need to do is send it to Amazon and voila, it works.

218
00:09:50,440 --> 00:09:51,520
 So the beautiful thing is,

219
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:53,800
 is the public keys can be distributed,

220
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:57,800
 they can be intercepted and it does not matter.

221
00:09:57,800 --> 00:10:02,680
 So with this notion of public private key encryption

222
00:10:02,680 --> 00:10:07,680
 in general, we made a change to HTTP.

223
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:12,000
 A layer, a mini layer is in the data model.

224
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,400
 If you remember way back,

225
00:10:13,400 --> 00:10:15,840
 perhaps you've forgotten about the layered model.

226
00:10:15,840 --> 00:10:17,840
 Remember that layered model?

227
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:20,800
 Application, transport, internet.

228
00:10:21,760 --> 00:10:23,920
 Remember this is sort of one computer

229
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:25,860
 and this is the other computer.

230
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:29,760
 These are the routers, routers, these are the hops.

231
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:30,800
 There's like 15 of these.

232
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:32,640
 Remember, remember all this?

233
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:34,760
 So it comes back not on us.

234
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:39,760
 Okay, so if you recall, just sort of to briefly remember

235
00:10:39,760 --> 00:10:42,400
 that the transport layer is responsible

236
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:43,920
 for the retransmission.

237
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:47,520
 It gives us the appearance of a reliable ordered connection

238
00:10:47,520 --> 00:10:52,060
 between our application and the bar application.

239
00:10:52,060 --> 00:10:55,200
 HTTP is one of the application protocols.

240
00:10:55,200 --> 00:10:58,160
 And so there is a little mini layer

241
00:10:58,160 --> 00:11:01,760
 that is layered in sort of seamlessly

242
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:04,080
 on top of the transport layer

243
00:11:04,080 --> 00:11:07,680
 that basically takes plaintext and encrypts it

244
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:09,520
 and turns it into ciphertext.

245
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:11,880
 And then ciphertext on the way out

246
00:11:11,880 --> 00:11:13,920
 and turns it back into plaintext.

247
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:15,840
 Okay, and so what happens is,

248
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:18,780
 is these applications just send plaintext

249
00:11:18,780 --> 00:11:20,320
 and out comes plaintext.

250
00:11:20,320 --> 00:11:22,880
 And there's a little bit of extra glue in the middle here

251
00:11:22,880 --> 00:11:26,760
 that's sort of a secure transport, secure sockets layer.

252
00:11:26,760 --> 00:11:29,880
 This is, this thing here is often called a socket.

253
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:33,700
 SOCK, it'd be good if I could spell socket.

254
00:11:33,700 --> 00:11:35,200
 So this is a socket.

255
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:38,760
 And then the red part is a secure socket.

256
00:11:38,760 --> 00:11:41,800
 So the applications kind of don't encrypt the data at all.

257
00:11:41,800 --> 00:11:44,120
 There is a library that encrypts it.

258
00:11:44,120 --> 00:11:46,600
 And the other thing is, is that all the rest of the internet,

259
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:50,040
 the internet, the link layer, the routers, nothing,

260
00:11:50,040 --> 00:11:52,560
 the ethernet, the fiber,

261
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:54,040
 they don't even know the difference

262
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:56,560
 between encrypted text and non-crypted text

263
00:11:56,560 --> 00:12:00,540
 because the encrypted text wanders around fully encrypted.

264
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:02,280
 Addresses are not encrypted.

265
00:12:02,280 --> 00:12:03,880
 And so it stays encrypted

266
00:12:03,880 --> 00:12:06,720
 all the way through the entire network.

267
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:09,800
 It actually, if you then think about the fact

268
00:12:09,800 --> 00:12:13,840
 that this is the moment that it leaves your computer,

269
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:16,120
 the only thing, so the plain text comes in here,

270
00:12:16,120 --> 00:12:18,840
 it gets encrypted here, encrypted comes down.

271
00:12:18,840 --> 00:12:21,920
 The only thing that leaves your computer is encrypted text.

272
00:12:21,920 --> 00:12:23,200
 And it makes it all the way across.

273
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:25,160
 The encrypted text goes into Amazon.

274
00:12:25,160 --> 00:12:26,620
 So this is Amazon.

275
00:12:26,620 --> 00:12:28,580
 This is you.

276
00:12:28,580 --> 00:12:33,560
 The encrypted text finds its way through all these things

277
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:35,460
 and it comes in encrypted.

278
00:12:35,460 --> 00:12:37,940
 And it actually doesn't get encrypted

279
00:12:37,940 --> 00:12:39,980
 until it's sort of right at the point

280
00:12:39,980 --> 00:12:41,320
 where Amazon's web server

281
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:43,500
 that's going to actually charge a credit card.

282
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:45,600
 So this is actually beautifully elegant

283
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:50,600
 in that the rest of the network is blissfully unaware

284
00:12:50,600 --> 00:12:55,360
 that any encryption is happening.

285
00:12:55,360 --> 00:12:56,660
 It's just moving the data.

286
00:12:56,660 --> 00:12:58,440
 So this did not require any change.

287
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:00,220
 Again, the beautiful layered architecture

288
00:13:00,220 --> 00:13:03,320
 did not require any change sort of below the transport layer.

289
00:13:03,320 --> 00:13:04,160
 And as a matter of fact,

290
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:06,440
 all of the sequencing and retransmission

291
00:13:06,440 --> 00:13:08,480
 that happens in the TCP layer,

292
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:10,520
 that happens with the encrypted stuff too,

293
00:13:10,520 --> 00:13:12,440
 because it's just encrypted.

294
00:13:12,440 --> 00:13:13,400
 It's just text.

295
00:13:13,400 --> 00:13:14,400
 It's gibberish text.

296
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:17,760
 It's not the original Visa card number that you're sending.

297
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:19,040
 You're sending one, two, three.

298
00:13:19,040 --> 00:13:21,520
 And out comes, you know, WXY.

299
00:13:21,520 --> 00:13:23,940
 The WXY just goes, it retransmitted.

300
00:13:23,940 --> 00:13:24,960
 All this crap just works.

301
00:13:24,960 --> 00:13:27,880
 It's like, beautiful.

302
00:13:27,880 --> 00:13:28,880
 It's a beautiful thing.

303
00:13:28,880 --> 00:13:31,180
 It's absolutely a beautiful thing.

304
00:13:31,180 --> 00:13:33,920
 And it's just like this mini layer kind of between the app.

305
00:13:33,920 --> 00:13:36,820
 It's like the top slice of the transport layer.

306
00:13:36,820 --> 00:13:38,560
 That's how I'm drawing it right here.

307
00:13:38,560 --> 00:13:41,200
 It's like this little kind of top extra little thing says,

308
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:42,880
 you know what, we're going to transport,

309
00:13:42,880 --> 00:13:44,800
 but actually help me out and give me some encryption

310
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:46,060
 while we're at it.

311
00:13:46,060 --> 00:13:48,240
 And there's all kinds of cool stuff that goes back and forth.

312
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:50,240
 The public and private keys get exchanged.

313
00:13:50,240 --> 00:13:52,600
 That's all kinds of stuff we don't worry about.

314
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:55,480
 We just send data and get data back.

315
00:13:55,480 --> 00:13:56,400
 Pretty cool, huh?

316
00:13:56,400 --> 00:14:01,480
 So this really solves the problem of the fact

317
00:14:01,480 --> 00:14:04,760
 that we basically should assume that everything

318
00:14:04,760 --> 00:14:08,200
 between our computer and the destination computer,

319
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:12,200
 this is you, this is Amazon, right?

320
00:14:13,280 --> 00:14:15,400
 Everything here, this is all dangerous.

321
00:14:15,400 --> 00:14:19,140
 There's some terrifyingly scary individual

322
00:14:19,140 --> 00:14:21,840
 that's watching everything, doing packet sniffing.

323
00:14:21,840 --> 00:14:25,080
 This might be Eve, the eavesdropper.

324
00:14:25,080 --> 00:14:26,600
 This looks like a little he.

325
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:28,640
 He looks pretty tough, right?

326
00:14:28,640 --> 00:14:30,760
 And so that even the wireless, right?

327
00:14:30,760 --> 00:14:32,240
 This is the Wi-Fi connection.

328
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:33,720
 The Wi-Fi is dangerous.

329
00:14:33,720 --> 00:14:36,680
 Now, the reality is these things aren't all that dangerous.

330
00:14:36,680 --> 00:14:39,600
 The Wi-Fi is probably the weakest link of this whole thing.

331
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:41,920
 But we have to assume that it's dangerous.

332
00:14:41,920 --> 00:14:44,960
 That's why we wanna assume that the only thing that's safe,

333
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:48,000
 and unfortunately, if you put viruses in your computer,

334
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:49,840
 then they can get at the plain text.

335
00:14:49,840 --> 00:14:52,520
 If Amazon loses its data somehow,

336
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:54,700
 then they get the plain text, right?

337
00:14:54,700 --> 00:14:59,700
 But basically, we wanna distrust all of this, okay?

338
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:05,520
 So this concept is called transport layer security,

339
00:15:05,520 --> 00:15:10,520
 also called SSL, also known as HTTPS, HTTPS.

340
00:15:12,840 --> 00:15:14,440
 For secure.

341
00:15:14,440 --> 00:15:16,640
 And it's kind of like between the TCP layer

342
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:17,480
 and the application layer,

343
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:19,200
 or the top half of the TCP layer

344
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:21,120
 is the way I like to think about it.

345
00:15:21,120 --> 00:15:24,480
 It's because it's based on public-private key encryption.

346
00:15:24,480 --> 00:15:27,480
 It's difficult, but not impossible.

347
00:15:27,480 --> 00:15:31,560
 Normal people don't have the kind of equipment to break it.

348
00:15:31,560 --> 00:15:34,080
 And even governments, if they can break it,

349
00:15:34,080 --> 00:15:35,840
 I don't even know, I'm not an expert.

350
00:15:35,840 --> 00:15:37,320
 I don't hang out with the government.

351
00:15:37,320 --> 00:15:38,640
 So I don't really know.

352
00:15:38,640 --> 00:15:41,960
 But assume that if they really put their mind to it

353
00:15:41,960 --> 00:15:44,400
 in a very narrow situation,

354
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:46,160
 if you become really interesting,

355
00:15:46,160 --> 00:15:47,720
 they will find your credit cards.

356
00:15:47,720 --> 00:15:50,200
 Probably there's easier ways to get your credit cards

357
00:15:50,200 --> 00:15:52,080
 than by decrypting your text.

358
00:15:52,080 --> 00:15:54,880
 So it's hard to decrypt.

359
00:15:54,880 --> 00:15:59,160
 And as I mentioned, because of the layered architecture,

360
00:15:59,160 --> 00:16:03,300
 the TCP layer, IP and link layers are completely unaware.

361
00:16:05,560 --> 00:16:10,560
 So you see this in the form of URLs that start with HTTPS.

362
00:16:10,560 --> 00:16:14,560
 They start with HTTPS,

363
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:19,280
 www.facebook.com versus HTTP.

364
00:16:19,280 --> 00:16:21,280
 And there was a time a few years back

365
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:25,760
 where it used to be a little more expensive

366
00:16:25,760 --> 00:16:28,720
 inside of the servers to do HTTPS.

367
00:16:28,720 --> 00:16:30,000
 It still is.

368
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:33,240
 And so some sites would try to do some of their activity

369
00:16:33,240 --> 00:16:35,520
 without using secure protocols.

370
00:16:35,520 --> 00:16:40,000
 And others would use non-secure and secure

371
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:41,080
 and then flip you back and forth

372
00:16:41,080 --> 00:16:42,880
 like if you're typing your password.

373
00:16:42,880 --> 00:16:44,440
 The problem was is that there was actually

374
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:47,820
 still sensitive data being sent even across the insecure.

375
00:16:47,820 --> 00:16:51,400
 And there was quite a famous thing

376
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:53,520
 where people could install a Firefox plugin

377
00:16:53,520 --> 00:16:57,240
 and watch Facebook, non-secure Facebook things

378
00:16:57,240 --> 00:17:00,460
 go back and forth across like a Starbucks.

379
00:17:00,460 --> 00:17:03,440
 And it would just show you all the people's Facebook accounts

380
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:05,560
 and you could log in as them and post as them.

381
00:17:05,560 --> 00:17:08,360
 And so you've seen a situation where companies

382
00:17:08,360 --> 00:17:11,400
 are just starting to use HTTPS for everything.

383
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:14,520
 You as a user have to be aware to see

384
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:16,240
 if you're typing anything sensitive,

385
00:17:16,240 --> 00:17:20,480
 never type it into a URL that doesn't say HTTPS.

386
00:17:20,480 --> 00:17:21,680
 Never do that.

387
00:17:21,680 --> 00:17:24,880
 You're typing a password, a credit card number,

388
00:17:24,880 --> 00:17:26,720
 any kind of personal information,

389
00:17:26,720 --> 00:17:29,600
 make sure you're doing HTTPS and make sure

390
00:17:29,600 --> 00:17:32,240
 that you know what it is.

391
00:17:32,240 --> 00:17:34,000
 We'll talk about that in a bit.

392
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:35,560
 We'll talk a little bit more about that in a bit.

393
00:17:35,560 --> 00:17:38,600
 So if we take a look and we think about

394
00:17:38,600 --> 00:17:39,840
 where the bad guys are at,

395
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:42,240
 the bad guys are kind of everything

396
00:17:42,240 --> 00:17:45,720
 and this secure TCP runs,

397
00:17:45,720 --> 00:17:48,720
 is the one part of the layer of architecture

398
00:17:48,720 --> 00:17:52,720
 that runs from within your laptop to within the server.

399
00:17:52,720 --> 00:17:55,560
 And so then if we kind of assume the worst,

400
00:17:55,560 --> 00:17:58,160
 the backbone is pretty safe.

401
00:17:58,160 --> 00:18:01,640
 The WiFi is probably the most dangerous, right?

402
00:18:01,640 --> 00:18:04,920
 But when we do secure system TCP,

403
00:18:04,920 --> 00:18:08,080
 secure system to system TCP,

404
00:18:08,080 --> 00:18:10,000
 we are doing the encryption

405
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,600
 right here inside your computer before it leaves.

406
00:18:12,600 --> 00:18:14,320
 And we're only doing the decryption

407
00:18:14,320 --> 00:18:15,840
 right when it comes back into computer.

408
00:18:15,840 --> 00:18:17,920
 So the decryption and encryption are happening

409
00:18:17,920 --> 00:18:21,400
 inside of Amazon's computer and inside of your computer

410
00:18:21,400 --> 00:18:22,880
 and nothing else.

411
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:25,440
 So secure sockets is pretty good.

412
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:27,800
 Now the place where you're still danger

413
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:31,760
 is there might be a virus that's watching your keystrokes.

414
00:18:31,760 --> 00:18:34,000
 This is why virus checking is so important

415
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,760
 because at some point you're typing it into your computer

416
00:18:36,760 --> 00:18:39,440
 and the greatest danger you have to losing your data

417
00:18:39,440 --> 00:18:42,640
 is really two things.

418
00:18:42,640 --> 00:18:44,440
 One, that you've got a virus

419
00:18:44,440 --> 00:18:47,680
 or B, somebody has redirected you

420
00:18:47,680 --> 00:18:51,800
 not to talk to evil Amazon instead of Amazon.

421
00:18:51,800 --> 00:18:55,440
 And that's what we'll talk about in the next lecture.

422
00:18:55,440 --> 00:18:57,880
 How does these browsers really know

423
00:18:57,880 --> 00:18:59,640
 that you're talking to the real Amazon?

424
00:18:59,640 --> 00:19:02,080
 And that is not confidentiality.

425
00:19:02,080 --> 00:19:04,440
 Confidentiality is stopping the bad guy

426
00:19:04,440 --> 00:19:07,800
 from seeing what you're sending as they're eavesdropping.

427
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:11,240
 Eve is eavesdropping, okay?

428
00:19:11,240 --> 00:19:13,840
 Now the next thing is the question of,

429
00:19:13,840 --> 00:19:17,600
 is this the real Amazon or is this a fake Amazon?

430
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:19,200
 So we'll talk about that next.

